<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bande Utkala Janani</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">written <a href="/years/1912">1912</a>; first sung at Balasore in <a href="/years/1912">1912</a>; state anthem of Odisha from 7 June <a href="/years/2020">2020</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as ବନ୍ଦେ ଉତ୍କଳ ଜନନୀ, Bandē Utkaḷa Jananī</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Bande Utkala Janani&quot; (&quot;Glory to the Mother Utkala!&quot;) is a patriotic poem in Odia written by Laxmikanta Mohapatra in 1912. Over the centuries outsiders, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> and the British, had ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a>, and its boundaries had changed with each of them. In the early 20th century a movement for a separate province gathered strength, led by the Utkal Sammilani, a federation of Odias.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Laxmikanta Mohapatra was young when he wrote the poem, and the campaign of the Utkal Sammilani for a separate Odia province had shaped his outlook. He wrote Bande Utkala Janani together with other fiery songs with a patriotic purpose. The poem imagines a Utkala that keeps her self-respect and dignity from a position of confidence and strength rather than insecurity and fear.</p><p>The song was first sung at the Utkal Sammilani's conference at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balasore">Balasore</a> in 1912, and the Sammilani used it in its campaign for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a>. The poet Banchhanidhi Mohanty, who was also a good singer, sang Laxmikanta's patriotic songs throughout Odisha. At a meeting at Bhadrak where Mohapatra was honoured, Harekrushna Mahtab, then a young Odia leader, said that the poet's songs had inspired him a great deal. Odisha became the first state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> to be formed on a linguistic basis on 1 April 1936.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The music was first recorded by Gokul Mohanty in 1932. In the early 1990s the Chief Minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a>, Biju Patnaik, asked Yudhistir Das, then Speaker of the Odisha Legislative Assembly, to have the song played at the end of the Assembly's sessions. In 1994 an all-party meeting presided over by Das agreed that it would be played and sung at the valedictory session of the Assembly.</p><p>In 2020 a state cabinet meeting chaired by Chief Minister Naveen Patnaik gave the poem the status of state anthem. The decision followed Patnaik's encouragement of recitations of Bande Utkala Janani to raise the morale of healthcare workers fighting the coronavirus outbreak. The anthem is sung to a modern notation arranged by the Odissi musician Balakrushna Dash.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bande Utkala Janani” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
